WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:05.250
With so much happening in The Rise of Skywalker,
there were definitely a few cool hidden details

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:05.760
you missed.

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:10.380
Fans would need their own wayfinders to track
it all down, not to mention decoding all the

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:12.700
characters with more meaning than you may
realize.

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:14.540
Here's what you may have overlooked.

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:20.890
Emperor Palpatine looks pretty rough post-resurrection
in The Rise of Skywalker, and understandably

00:00:20.890 --> 00:00:23.460
so, but his majestic throne is a different
story.

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:28.310
Palp's craggy chair, "The Throne of the Sith,"
is the coolest bad guy seat outside of Game

00:00:28.310 --> 00:00:30.180
of Thrones, and there's a good reason for
that.

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:35.260
The legendary Ralph McQuarrie, the man largely
responsible for the look of the original Star

00:00:35.269 --> 00:00:39.390
Wars trilogy, designed it in the '80s for
Return of the Jedi.

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:43.739
Lucasfilm creative art manager Phil Szostak
revealed this unused throne room art on Twitter

00:00:43.739 --> 00:00:47.999
back when the final Rise of Skywalker trailer
dropped, posting McQuarrie's old sketches

00:00:47.999 --> 00:00:50.170
next to Palpatine's new throne.

00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:54.930
McQuarrie passed away in 2012, and his obituary
in the Guardian notes that "fewer of his ideas

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:56.739
made the cut" in Return of the Jedi.

00:00:56.739 --> 00:01:01.570
So it's cool to see McQuarrie's work put to
excellent use in Skywalker.

00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:05.960
Diehard fans know that Darth Vader's helmet,
designed by Ralph McQuarrie, was inspired

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:10.560
by samurai armor, and director J.J. Abrams
made it clear to Entertainment Weekly in 2015

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:15.330
that Vader's grandson Kylo Ren's helmet is
"meant to be a nod to [Vader's]," in case

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:16.500
that wasn't obvious enough.

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:22.000
Ren's helmet, smashed to bits by its owner
in The Last Jedi, is back in The Rise of Skywalker,

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:26.270
but only after some repairs that also evoke
a centuries-old Japanese tradition.

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:31.480
In Skywalker, an unnamed ape-like creature
restores Ren's helmet using a technique similar

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:33.220
to kintsukuroi, or "golden repair."

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:38.180
Kintsukuroi is all about embracing flaws as
aesthetic features, and typically involves

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:40.580
repairing broken pottery using visible gold
seams.

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:46.270
In The Rise of Skywalker, Ren's helmet is
reforged using a red material of some kind,

00:01:46.270 --> 00:01:47.420
but the idea is the same.

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:51.520
Rather than make the helmet repair seamless,
the red becomes part of the new design.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:56.490
Abrams confirmed that kintsukuroi was the
inspiration for Ren's Rise of Skywalker helmet

00:01:56.490 --> 00:01:58.120
in an interview with Empire, saying,

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:02.420
"Having [Kylo Ren] be masked, but also fractured,
is a very intentional thing.

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:07.460
Like that classic Japanese process of taking
ceramics and repairing them [...] As fractured

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:11.269
as Ren is, the mask becomes a visual representation
of that.

00:02:11.269 --> 00:02:13.439
There's something about this that tells his
history.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:16.580
His mask doesn't ultimately hide him and his
behavior is revealed."

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:22.300
When Rey uses her Force powers to heal the
underground sand snake on Pasaana, thus ensuring

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:27.300
a safe exit for Episode IX's heroes, it was
surely a surprise to casual fans of the Skywalker

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:27.840
saga.

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:31.580
We've seen the power of the Force do some
pretty insane stuff, but it's mostly used

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:35.540
for telekinesis and "mind tricks" at least
in the eight films leading up to The Rise

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:36.480
of Skywalker.

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:38.519
"Does she do that to us?"

00:02:38.519 --> 00:02:42.640
In the Expanded Universe, Force healing is
definitely a thing, but Episode IX is the

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:45.560
first big-screen depiction of the Force being
used in such a manner.

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:47.380
The small screen, however, is a different
matter.

00:02:48.069 --> 00:02:52.420
Baby Yoda attempts to heal Mando in Chapter
2 of the Disney Plus series The Mandalorian,

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:55.280
but is inadvertently stopped just short of
doing so.

00:02:55.280 --> 00:03:00.320
In Chapter 7, however, released on the streaming
service the day before Skywalker hit theaters,

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:04.520
everyone's favorite 50-year-old baby heals
Bounty Hunters' Guild head Greef Karga after

00:03:04.529 --> 00:03:08.730
he's scratched up and poisoned by pterodactyl
-like creatures on the lava-spewing planet

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:09.320
Nevarro.

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:13.740
Like the sand snake in Skywalker, Karga is
so grateful that he decides to let our heroes

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:14.620
escape with their lives.

00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:19.560
Fans of The Mandalorian likely perked up when
they saw Rey (and later Kylo Ren) pulling

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:24.160
off the same miraculous trick a neat reward
for those who caught the episode the day before.

00:03:25.049 --> 00:03:29.460
After Ben Solo gets chucked into a seemingly
bottomless pit by Emperor Palpatine just like

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:34.240
Anakin did to Palpatine in Episode VI a devastated
Rey, who still has the Emperor and his deadly

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:38.500
Force lightning to contend with, meditates
and hears several voices.

00:03:39.230 --> 00:03:44.260
There's no way those voices are just random,
of course, and some, like Yoda, Anakin, Mace

00:03:44.279 --> 00:03:48.830
Windu, Obi-Wan Kenobi, and Qui-Gonn Jinn,
are pretty obvious, but in the chaos of the

00:03:48.830 --> 00:03:51.480
big final battle it was probably hard to make
all of them out.

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:55.960
Here's a quick rundown of the more obscure
"Voices of Jedi Past" featured in this sequence:

00:03:56.680 --> 00:04:00.999
Adi Gallia first appeared in The Phantom Menace,
but most fans know her best from the animated

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:01.820
Star Wars: The Clone Wars series.

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:08.200
Similarly, Luminara Unduli and Aayla Secura
first appeared in Attack of the Clones, but

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:09.440
are both best known from Clone Wars.

00:04:10.249 --> 00:04:14.900
Ahsoka Tano is also from Clone Wars, but The
Rise of Skywalker is the character's live-action

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:17.160
debut, technically, even if we only hear her
voice.

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:20.180
Same goes for Kanan Jarrus from Star Wars
Rebels.

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:25.300
Yep: that means Freddie Prinze Jr. or his
voice, at least is in The Rise of Skywalker

00:04:25.820 --> 00:04:26.600
"You're serious?"

00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:27.600
"As a heart attack."

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:32.590
The Rise of Skywalker ends with the heroes
of the new trilogy celebrating in much the

00:04:32.590 --> 00:04:36.730
same way the original trilogy's heroes did
in Return of the Jedi, prefaced with much

00:04:36.730 --> 00:04:38.070
intergalactic fanfare.

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:42.140
But this time around, one overlooked Wookie
finally gets the gold.

00:04:42.140 --> 00:04:46.750
In arguably the most blatant example of fan
service in the film, Chewbacca, who famously

00:04:46.750 --> 00:04:51.650
did not get the same recognition as the humans
during the Royal Award Ceremony in A New Hope,

00:04:51.650 --> 00:04:56.200
gets a medal from Maz Kanata in The Rise of
Skywalker and it's not just any medal.

00:04:56.200 --> 00:05:00.500
It's heavily implied that this award, which
Leia clutches as she passes away, is the one

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:01.780
Han received in A New Hope.

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:06.800
In that first Star Wars installment, you'll
remember, Chewie stands quietly in the background

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:08.600
while Princess Leia hangs medals on Han and
Luke.

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:13.320
His untranslated cry is the last sound you
hear in the film, and for Chewie fans, it

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:15.600
might as well have been subtitled, "What about
me?"

00:05:16.130 --> 00:05:20.160
Fan service or not, there's no denying that
the big lug deserved it, considering all that

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:20.720
he's been through.

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:28.200
In The Rise of Skywalker, the snowy planet
of Kijimi plays a key role in the adventure

00:05:28.210 --> 00:05:33.860
as the home planet of Zorii Bliss, who ultimately
helps get C-3PO to Babu Frik, the cute alien

00:05:33.860 --> 00:05:36.960
dude who hacks his programming in order to
translate the Sith dagger.

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:38.720
So what's with the name "Kijimi"?

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:43.900
Well, thanks to one observant Redditor, we
know that it's the name of a synthesizer manufactured

00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:48.300
by Black Corporation, a Japanese company who
counts Skywalker director J.J. Abrams as a

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:49.390
huge fan.

00:05:49.390 --> 00:05:53.650
So it makes sense that legendary composer
John Williams, fresh off his ninth and probably

00:05:53.650 --> 00:05:58.610
final Star Wars composing gig, appears in
a brief cameo on the synth-inspired planet.

00:05:58.610 --> 00:06:02.710
It's a quick, blink-and-you-miss it moment,
but he's there, wearing a futuristic monocle

00:06:02.710 --> 00:06:06.020
and shaking his head at our heroes as they
enter the droidsmith's shop.

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:10.620
Bonus fun fact: His character's name is "Oma
Tres," an anagram of "Maestro" .

00:06:11.780 --> 00:06:15.840
X-wing pilot Wedge Antilles was one of the
heroes of The Battle of Yavin in A New Hope,

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:18.960
narrowly surviving the daring attack on the
Death Star 1.0.

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:23.460
He appeared again in The Empire Strikes Back
and Return of the Jedi, but The Rise of Skywalker

00:06:23.560 --> 00:06:26.180
marks his first appearance in the new trilogy.

00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:30.820
Actor Denis Lawson reprises the role in a
brief cameo in the final battle praising Lando's

00:06:30.820 --> 00:06:33.750
aim, to boot! but it sounds like he needed
some convincing.

00:06:33.750 --> 00:06:37.960
In 2015, Lawson told The Courier that he was
offered a role in The Force Awakens, but,

00:06:37.960 --> 00:06:40.400
believe it or not, he flat-out turned it down.

00:06:40.870 --> 00:06:44.960
When asked about the potential gig at a screening
for The Machine, Lawson told a reporter he

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:46.780
had no interest in the role whatsoever.

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:48.470
The Scottish actor said:

00:06:48.470 --> 00:06:49.710
"I'm not going to do that.

00:06:49.710 --> 00:06:51.420
[Disney] asked me but it just would have bored
me."

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:56.610
We can't say for sure at this time what convinced
Lawson to don Wedge's uniform once again for

00:06:56.610 --> 00:07:01.010
Skywalker, but our guess is that Disney was
perhaps a bit more generous with the galactic

00:07:01.010 --> 00:07:02.800
credits, if you catch our drift.

00:07:03.820 --> 00:07:08.380
As befitting the ninth film in an epic saga,
The Rise of Skywalker is positively packed

00:07:08.680 --> 00:07:12.840
with nods to past installments, some a bit
less significant than others, but still cool.

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:17.520
Among the many mini-Easter eggs and callbacks
hidden within: Rey's yellow lightsaber at

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:18.080
the end.

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:21.940
Clone Wars and Rebels feature ones of that
hue, and there's an old official toy saber

00:07:22.180 --> 00:07:25.480
that's yellow, but it's the first of its kind
in the Skywalker saga.

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:30.200
Also, Ben and Han's implied "I love you/I
know" exchange is a callback to the famous

00:07:30.200 --> 00:07:32.200
Han and Leia scene in The Empire Strikes Back,
of course.

00:07:33.060 --> 00:07:38.080
There's so much more: C-3PO's line about the
Aki-Aki celebration only happening "every

00:07:38.200 --> 00:07:42.580
42 years" is a sly reference to the fact that
when The Rise of Skywalker was released, A

00:07:42.580 --> 00:07:44.380
New Hope was 42 years old.

00:07:44.980 --> 00:07:49.330
Lando's blaster is the same model as the one
he used in Solo, which is a neat detail.

00:07:49.330 --> 00:07:50.340
The "Holdo Maneuver"?

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:53.560
A reference to Vice Admiral Holdo's daring
trick in The Last Jedi.

00:07:53.850 --> 00:07:57.780
We could go on and on, but some of the best
references in The Rise of Skywalker actually

00:07:57.780 --> 00:07:59.900
came in the form of cool character cameos.

00:08:01.020 --> 00:08:04.320
"I guess we'll have to eat this boring oatmeal.”

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:05.360
“It's a trap!”

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:07.500
“Admiral Ackbar!"

00:08:07.640 --> 00:08:11.730
Admiral Ackbar is more than just a meme; He
was a staunch ally of the Republic during

00:08:11.730 --> 00:08:16.170
the Clone Wars, one of the Rebel Alliance's
most valued leaders during the Galactic Civil

00:08:16.170 --> 00:08:18.770
War, and one of the co-founders of the Resistance.

00:08:18.770 --> 00:08:23.370
He was also a brilliant tactician, a former
prisoner of war, and a family man.

00:08:23.370 --> 00:08:27.340
The last bit came to light in Marvel Comics'
Star Wars: Allegiance, which bridges part

00:08:27.340 --> 00:08:29.860
of the gap between The Last Jedi and The Rise
of Skywalker.

00:08:30.440 --> 00:08:34.360
The Resistance forces are decimated after
the Battle of Crait, and Admiral Ackbar is

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:35.660
among the casualties of war.

00:08:36.140 --> 00:08:40.660
Leia needs new ships, so she heads to Admiral
Ackbar's home planet, Mon Cala, to recruit

00:08:40.660 --> 00:08:43.520
some new allies, where she's greeted by his
son, Aftab.

00:08:44.229 --> 00:08:48.200
Aftab helps Leia foil the plot of a Quarren
terrorist, assists in her fight against the

00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:51.640
First Order, and forges a new treaty to get
the Resistance the help it needs.

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:56.670
By Episode 9, he's joined the Resistance in
full, offering advice during some of the briefing

00:08:56.670 --> 00:08:58.180
scenes at Resistance HQ.

00:08:59.240 --> 00:09:03.860
Allegiant General Pryde, played by Academy
Award nominee Richard E. Grant, makes quite

00:09:03.860 --> 00:09:04.420
an impression.

00:09:04.600 --> 00:09:08.640
Not only is he a sour, foreboding officer
in the same vein as Peter Cushing's beloved

00:09:08.700 --> 00:09:13.260
Grand Moff Tarkin, but by the end of the film
he's Kylo Ren's right-hand man, having replaced

00:09:13.620 --> 00:09:15.700
General Hux during his rise to power.

00:09:15.820 --> 00:09:19.320
The movie doesn't share much about Pryde's
past, but we learn late in the film that he

00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:21.380
also served the Empire under Emperor Palpatine.

00:09:22.190 --> 00:09:27.000
According to official Star Wars lore, he also
helped train, and apparently kidnap, the First

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:28.330
Order's legions of Stormtroopers.

00:09:29.060 --> 00:09:31.860
He's been around for a long time, and he's
down with the Sith.

00:09:31.980 --> 00:09:33.500
"No going to the Dark side.”

00:09:33.500 --> 00:09:34.100
“Okay!"

00:09:34.860 --> 00:09:39.060
If you were watching TV or movies in the 2000s,
you'll recognize actor Dominic Monaghan right

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:39.620
away.

00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:43.860
The English actor played the hobbit Merry
in The Lord of the Rings, and had a leading

00:09:43.860 --> 00:09:46.700
role on Lost, which was co-created by J.J.
Abrams.

00:09:47.240 --> 00:09:51.220
While Abrams was writing The Rise of Skywalker,
Abrams and Monaghan bet on a World Cup soccer

00:09:51.220 --> 00:09:51.780
game.

00:09:52.280 --> 00:09:53.040
The prize?

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:54.040
A role in Episode 9.

00:09:54.680 --> 00:09:58.160
Obviously, Monaghan won the wager, and Beaumont
Kin was born.

00:09:58.440 --> 00:10:02.230
Abrams might've created Beaumont for his friend,
but he didn't give the character very much

00:10:02.230 --> 00:10:02.760
to do on screen.

00:10:03.320 --> 00:10:06.280
Thankfully, a couple of comics have fleshed
out Kin's personality.

00:10:06.860 --> 00:10:12.360
In Star Wars Adventures #27 through #29, Kin
teams up with Chewbacca to liberate the Wookiee's

00:10:12.360 --> 00:10:13.600
homeworld from the First Order.

00:10:14.200 --> 00:10:18.240
We learn that Kin fancies himself a historian,
although he's not quite as smart as he thinks

00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:18.800
he is.

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:22.150
In fact, overcompensating seems to be Kin's
whole deal.

00:10:22.150 --> 00:10:27.120
He pretends to know Chewie, a war hero, better
than he does, and despite his brave façade

00:10:27.120 --> 00:10:27.660
he's scared of ghosts.

00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:31.500
Still, when push comes to shove, Beaumont
rises to the occasion.

00:10:32.840 --> 00:10:37.270
That's not just any Ewok watching happily
as a First Order Star Destroyer blows up above

00:10:37.270 --> 00:10:38.360
the forest moon of Endor.

00:10:39.220 --> 00:10:43.380
That's Wicket W. Warrick, the first Ewok that
Leia met in Return of the Jedi.

00:10:47.260 --> 00:10:48.080
"I'm not gonna hurt you."

00:10:48.760 --> 00:10:53.330
It's even the same actor in the suit: Willow
and Leprechaun star Warwick Davis suited up

00:10:53.330 --> 00:10:57.100
again for The Rise of Skywalker, returning
to the role that he originated when he was

00:10:57.100 --> 00:10:58.140
just 11 years old.

00:10:58.960 --> 00:11:03.330
Wicket was arguably Return of the Jedi's biggest
breakout star, and he went on to become one

00:11:03.330 --> 00:11:05.880
of the most widely featured Star Wars characters
throughout the '80s.

00:11:07.050 --> 00:11:11.180
Davis played the role two more times in two
made-for-TV movies, as well as a long list

00:11:11.180 --> 00:11:13.300
of additional Star Wars characters over the
years.

00:11:14.080 --> 00:11:18.980
What you may not have noticed, however, is
that Wicket now has a son: Pommet, who's played

00:11:18.980 --> 00:11:21.060
by Warwick Davis' own teenage son, Harrison.

00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:23.980
